<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro Santana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro Santana was a Dominican rancher and general who led the army in the war of independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in 1844 and became the first president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>. He alternated in power with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenaventura-baez">Buenaventura Báez</a> and in 1861 arranged the country's annexation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, serving as governor until the War of Restoration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-santana.jpg" alt="Pedro Santana" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Epifanio Billini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_Santana_Dominican_Republic_president_1800s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Santana was born on 29 June 1801 in Hincha, in the Captaincy General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, to Pedro Santana and Petronila Familias, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herders (hateros) of Canarian origin who moved east for safety and settled at last in El Seibo. He had a twin brother, Ramón. Through his marriage to the widow of his father's partner, Miguel Febles, the brothers gained moderate wealth and great influence in El Seibo, though neither had any education.</p><p>Like the other hateros he opposed Haitian rule, which began in 1822. Approached by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-pablo-duarte">Juan Pablo Duarte</a>'s La Trinitaria in 1843, he agreed to join the plan for independence on condition that he command, though he did not share the political beliefs of the Trinitarios.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After independence was proclaimed on 27 February 1844 he led the southern army and defeated the forces of the Haitian president Hérard at the Battle of Azua on 19 March. He then overthrew the Central Governing Board. Article 210 of the 1844 Constitution, which he demanded, made him unaccountable for his actions, and he used it to execute opponents, among them María Trinidad Sánchez in 1845 and José Joaquín Puello in 1847. He resigned on 4 August 1848.</p><p>Recalled to face the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faustin-soulouque">Faustin Soulouque</a>, he won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-carreras">Battle of Las Carreras</a> on 21 April 1849 and was named Liberator of the Fatherland. He returned to the presidency in 1853 and resigned in 1856. Deported to Martinique by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenaventura-baez">Buenaventura Báez</a> in 1857, he came back to lead the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, and in 1858 deposed José Desiderio Valverde and took the presidency a third time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Santana negotiated the country's reintegration into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, under terms that made him head of the local administration and promised that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> would not be restored, and he exiled or imprisoned nationalists who had fought beside him. In 1862 Queen Isabella II made him Marquess of Las Carreras. He died on 14 June 1864 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-restoration-war">Dominican Restoration War</a> and was buried in the Fortaleza Ozama. He is considered a gifted strategist, but historians such as Nancie L. González and Howard J. Wiarda think acts such as the execution of Antonio Duvergé in 1855 barred him from becoming a national hero.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pedro Santana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The cattle rancher <b>Pedro Santana</b> defeated the Haitian army and soon exiled Duarte and the other founders.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-dominican-republic">First Dominican Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Facing debt, political rivalry and the threat of invasion from Haiti, President <b>Pedro Santana</b> arranged for the First Dominican Republic to be annexed by Spain, proclaimed on 18 March 1861.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-santo-domingo-1861-1865">Province of Santo Domingo (1861-1865)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hinche, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/dominican-republic">the Dominican Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.15,-72.02&amp;year=1841" aria-label="Hinche on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pedro Santana"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4036 5872h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4036 5872h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.15,-72.02&amp;year=1841">What was here in 1841? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
